Schools of life: desire, agency, and doubt (@greek-schools-life: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @ancient-greek-philosophy, #contains @greek-epicurus, #contains @greek-epictetus, #contains @greek-sceptics, #contains @greek-cynics, #contains @greek-plotinus, #philosophy @greek-cynics, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-cynics, #philosophy @greek-epictetus, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-epictetus, #philosophy @greek-epicurus, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-epicurus, #philosophy @greek-plotinus, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-plotinus, #philosophy @greek-sceptics, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-sceptics) { r_58ENCWSNQARKCDXEP8FVWY9507
How can philosophical inquiry change a life? Follow competing approaches through surviving texts and ancient reports. These readings include later Greek-speaking authors in the Roman world as well as Hellenistic schools.

| Path | Read | Useful comparison |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Practice, simplicity, and convention | [The Cynics](Cynics.lingua#r_0R2YCT14QC77FB489JPCD107SZ) | Socrates' examined life and the material conditions of self-sufficiency |
| Pleasure, desire, and the fear of death | [Epicurus](Epicurus.lingua#r_6KHZKEFRKY1J18Z7TSRAJABZQP) | Gorgias on desire; Phaedo on death |
| Judgment, agency, and what depends on us | [Epictetus](Epictetus.lingua#r_35EWPVCFYNWZ0DYG5R5HM05TC2) | Crito on action; Aristotle on practical wisdom |
| Conflicting accounts and suspension of judgment | [The sceptics](Sceptics.lingua#r_6KEA1AHWJRDT3ZN8VJQTK06ZFK) | Socratic questioning and Meno's inquiry difficulty |
| Beauty, intelligibility, and a later Platonism | [Plotinus](Plotinus.lingua#r_375WTA2HXW04F1FC4FVW20B9N9) | Symposium's account of ascent and the role of an analogy |

**Entry:** reconstruct a claim and its reasons with [the reading method](../foundations/Read-an-argument.lingua#r_4GMQCKPBF9BCSKDK0KPN013EBN). Read the relevant earlier argument alongside when making a comparison; the complete Socratic path is not required.

**Outcome:** compare two accounts of how to live and explain how each connects an ethical recommendation to claims about nature, knowledge, or agency. An exercise in trying an idea can reveal a difficulty; a pleasant experience alone cannot establish the whole doctrine.

**Return:** [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3). **Sources:** [Books and access](../sources/Sources.lingua#r_6R0NA5RTH31HV871Y0RMF96WX3).

**Sustained study:** use [the Hellenistic disputes](../research/seminars/Hellenistic-disputes.lingua#r_7020B909F5EN8WYCK7HZ0F1ZBV) and [the later Platonism seminar](../research/seminars/Later-Platonism.lingua#r_1CGS6HJE7MYDP4WEZG2GYYR769) to connect the introductory readings with Greek or Latin evidence, critical scholarship, and independent arguments.
} r_58ENCWSNQARKCDXEP8FVWY9507
